    "text": "Build as you already work Keep working with your conversational coding agent. Explain the change, discuss tradeoffs, inspect the implementation and decide what should happen next. With AIDA configured in the project, your agent can use its CLI or MCP tools to maintain a durable record of requirements, decisions and the work that fulfills them. The result is the system you developed and a record of its intent: why it behaves that way, what was agreed and where the implementation came from. That record helps you maintain and enhance the system and work with other people and agents. 1. Install AIDA AIDA is alpha software and supports Linux (x86_64 and arm64). Choose whichever installation path fits your workflow: hand a prompt to your coding agent, install a prebuilt binary from a release host, or build from source code. Option A: Give your coding agent an install prompt If you are already inside Claude Code, Codex CLI, or Antigravity, paste this prompt so your agent follows the installation instructions and operator setup guide for you: Install AIDA on this Linux machine by following https://github.com/joemooney/aida#install (README.md#install and docs/operator-setup.md). Inspect scripts/install.sh first, install the latest release binary into ~/.local/bin (or build from source if we have a local checkout), verify aida --version is on PATH, and then run aida init --help and aida init in this repository. Ask me before changing shell profile files or enabling optional --with-mcp registration. Option B: Install the prebuilt binary from a release location For end users and CI runners (no Rust toolchain required), the release installer detects your Linux architecture (x86_64 or arm64), downloads the release tarball (aida-linux-x86_64.tar.gz or aida-linux-arm64.tar.gz), and installs aida, aida-server, aida-porcelain, and the aida-flow symlink into ~/.local/bin/: bash Install the latest release from GitHub Releases into ~/.local/bin curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/joemooney/aida/main/scripts/install.sh | bash Pin a specific release tag or choose a custom install directory curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/joemooney/aida/main/scripts/install.sh | bash -s -- --version v0.16.0 --prefix ~/.local/bin Or install from a GitLab release host (or set AIDA_RELEASE_HOST=gitlab) curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/joemooney/aida/main/scripts/install.sh | bash -s -- --host gitlab Ensure ~/.local/bin is on your PATH, then verify and update later with: bash aida --version aida upgrade Option C: Build and install from source code If you have a stable Rust toolchain and want to build directly from source (no external protoc binary is needed \u2014 protobuf definitions compile with the pure-Rust protox crate): bash git clone https://github.com/joemooney/aida.git cd aida Build and install aida + aida-porcelain + aida-flow into ~/.cargo/bin make install-cli (or run make install to also build and install aida-server) You can also run the underlying Cargo commands directly: bash cargo install --path aida-cli cargo install --path aida-porcelain ln -sfn aida-porcelain \"${CARGO_INSTALL_ROOT:-${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}}/bin/aida-flow\" aida --version 2. Initialize or join a repository Once aida is on your PATH, initialize AIDA inside your Git repository: bash aida init --help aida init The full footprint scaffolds project guidance and agent skills for selected profiles; --agent chooses profiles explicitly. The CLI is the default tool surface for agents with shell access. MCP registration is opt-in with --with-mcp; aida mcp-serve provides the stdio server for MCP clients. Check the client setup matrix for your harness. Agent permissions, project policy and review authority still need deliberate configuration. aida init also installs Git commit hooks. By default they do not refuse a commit because of its message. If a commit subject does not follow the type(scope): summary (TASK-12) style AIDA uses to link commits to specs, the hook prints one hint: line. To skip the hooks, run aida init --no-hooks. Operators can follow the supported setup and storage guide. Before creating specs in a fresh clone of an existing AIDA project, run aida init (or aida node acquire) in the clone to establish its local identity. Follow the project's onboarding guidance and preserve existing adoption/configuration choices. Operators can consult the setup reference and storage reference. The first-project walkthrough is a detailed command-oriented exercise for operators and agents; ordinary users can start with the conversational journey below. 3. Start with a conversation Tell your AIDA-aware agent what you want to build. For example: Add a way to export invoices. Before implementing it, record the agreed behavior and acceptance criteria in AIDA. Ask me about choices that affect customers. You remain the developer. Explore alternatives, change your mind and review the result in your usual editor and pull-request workflow. The agent uses AIDA commands to read existing intent, capture agreed requirements, record decisions and connect changes to the relevant specs. You do not need to memorize requirement-mutation commands to begin. 4. Make the record part of the work Ask the agent to show what it recorded before implementation. Correct missing constraints or misunderstood intent. As implementation reveals new facts, ask it to update the record and explain material changes. At a stopping point, ask: \"What intent did we capture, what did we implement, and what is still unresolved?\" The capture skill can help an agent sweep the conversation for missed requirements. Capture depends on the configured agent following the project guidance and invoking the tools; AIDA does not automatically record every utterance or observe work outside those tools. 5. Return with context Later, ask the agent to find the requirement or decision behind a behavior before changing it. Specs, comments, relationships and code traces provide starting points for maintenance and enhancement. A colleague can use the same project record to understand the agreed behavior and contribute a change. The record needs care: check that acceptance criteria match the intended outcome and that the implementation and links remain current. Unrecorded decisions cannot be recovered from the store by assumption. 6. Choose what to delegate You can work conversationally throughout. Autonomous delivery is an option for suitable authorized work, not a prerequisite for onboarding. People can develop, review and collaborate while agents handle tracking and selected implementation steps. Execution mode and authority are separate: selecting a mode does not grant permission to approve or merge. Read the AIDA overview for selectable autonomy and current limitations. AIDA is alpha software and supports Linux only. Uninstall Back up and publish any intent history you need before removing AIDA. Remove the binary from the location where you installed it, and remove AIDA's client registration and generated guidance only after checking for local customizations. Keep your source repository and any store history you still need. For a live dev installation, inspect aida dev status first so you identify the active links and binaries correctly.",
